CAT = "cemapAdvice"; SEG = "ADV"
Q = [
("Which is an example of 'hard facts' gathered in a fact find?",
 "Gross annual income", ["Attitude to risk", "Plans to retire early", "Feelings about debt"],
 "Hard facts are objective and checkable, such as income, age, debts and outgoings. Attitudes, priorities and plans are soft facts."),
("Which is an example of 'soft facts' gathered in a fact find?",
 "The client's priorities", ["Date of birth", "Monthly loan payments", "Current mortgage balance"],
 "Soft facts are the client's views, feelings, priorities and plans. They are harder to measure but just as important to a suitable recommendation."),
("Which question is an open question?",
 "How do you feel about debt?", ["Are you employed full time?", "Do you have any children?", "Is your income over budget?"],
 "Open questions invite a full answer and draw out soft facts. Closed questions, answerable yes or no, are useful for confirming hard facts."),
("Repeating back a summary of what the client has said, to check it is understood, is an example of:",
 "Active listening", ["Closed questioning", "Upselling", "Execution-only sales"],
 "Summarising and reflecting back shows the client they have been heard and confirms the adviser has understood. It is part of good communication."),
("Why should an adviser identify a client's needs before discussing products?",
 "So the advice fits the client", ["So the firm can earn more fees", "So no fact find is needed", "So the client signs sooner"],
 "Needs come first; products are then matched to them. Starting from a product risks a sale that does not suit the client."),
("A client's capacity for loss refers to:",
 "Ability to absorb falls in value", ["How much they want to invest", "Their emotional attitude to risk", "The size of their cash deposit"],
 "Capacity for loss is a financial measure: how far the client's lifestyle would suffer if investments fell. Attitude to risk is how comfortable they feel with risk."),
("Before making a recommendation, an adviser checks a client's stated income against payslips. This is part of:",
 "Verifying the information", ["Building rapport with the client", "Closing the sale", "Researching products"],
 "Advisers check that what the client says is plausible and, where needed, evidenced. Income evidence is central to responsible mortgage lending."),
("Which is the best order for the advice process?",
 "Gather facts, analyse, recommend, review", ["Recommend, gather facts, analyse, review", "Analyse, recommend, review, gather facts", "Review, gather facts, recommend, analyse"],
 "Advice starts with information, then analysis of needs and options, then a recommendation, followed by ongoing review where agreed."),
("An 'insistent client' is one who:",
 "Goes ahead against the advice", ["Asks a great many questions", "Refuses to give any information", "Keeps changing to a new adviser"],
 "An insistent client chooses a different course from the recommendation. The adviser must make the risks clear and record that the client is acting against advice."),
("When a client insists on a transaction the adviser has advised against, the adviser should:",
 "Record the advice and the choice", ["Change the advice to match theirs", "Refuse ever to deal with them again", "Report the client to the FCA"],
 "Clear records show the advice given, the risks explained and the client's own decision. The transaction must not be presented as the adviser's recommendation."),
("A suitability report sent after advice mainly explains:",
 "Why the advice suits the client", ["The firm's annual profit figures", "How to complain to the FCA", "The adviser's sales targets"],
 "It links the recommendation to the client's needs, objectives and circumstances, and sets out risks and any disadvantages."),
("Body language that is most likely to help build rapport with a client is:",
 "Open posture and eye contact", ["Arms folded across the chest", "Checking a phone often", "Looking at the clock a lot"],
 "Open posture, steady eye contact and attentive expressions put clients at ease. Closed or distracted signals make clients less willing to share information."),
("Case: Ravi, 34, wants to remortgage and also mentions he has no life cover and two young children. What should the adviser do?",
 "Raise the protection need with him", ["Ignore it, as he asked about a mortgage", "Sell cover without a fact find", "Refer him to the FCA"],
 "A full fact find covers all relevant needs. Pointing out an obvious protection gap is part of good advice, even if the client came in for a mortgage."),
("Case: Ravi, 34, wants to remortgage and says his bonus is guaranteed, but his contract shows it is discretionary. How should the adviser treat the bonus?",
 "Cautiously, as it may not be paid", ["As fully guaranteed income", "As irrelevant to the advice", "As a reason to stop advising him"],
 "Advisers rely on evidence, not just what the client says. A discretionary bonus may not be paid, so it is treated cautiously in affordability."),
("Case: Ravi, 34, plans to start a family soon and his partner may stop work for two years. Why does this matter to the mortgage advice?",
 "Future income may fall", ["It changes his credit score", "Lenders ignore future plans", "It removes the need for advice"],
 "Advice must consider likely changes in circumstances. A planned drop in household income affects how much is affordable and which product suits."),
("Case: Ravi, 34, decides to remortgage and asks for a recommendation by email the same day. What should the adviser make sure of first?",
 "Enough information is gathered", ["The quickest product is chosen", "No fact find is recorded", "The highest fee is charged"],
 "Speed never replaces a proper fact find. The recommendation must rest on enough information to show it is suitable."),
("A recommendation should be reviewed when:",
 "Circumstances change", ["The adviser changes firm", "The client pays a fee", "A new tax year starts"],
 "A change such as a new job, divorce or a birth can make earlier advice unsuitable. Reviews keep the solution matched to the client."),
("If an adviser does not have the expertise to advise on a client's need, they should:",
 "Refer the client to a specialist", ["Advise anyway to keep the client", "Guess the best product", "Tell the client the need is not real"],
 "Advisers must work within their competence. Referring to a qualified specialist protects the client and the adviser."),
("Which shows the adviser is analysing rather than just collecting information?",
 "Comparing needs with existing cover", ["Writing down the client's address", "Asking for the client's date of birth", "Photocopying a passport"],
 "Analysis means working out the gap between what the client has and what they need. Collecting details is only the first stage."),
("Case: Mei, 58, wants to borrow over 30 years and plans to retire at 67. What should the adviser check?",
 "How she will pay after retiring", ["Whether she owns her own car", "Her favourite lender's branding", "Her employer's profit last year"],
 "A term running past retirement needs evidence that payments are affordable from retirement income. Responsible lending rules expect this to be considered."),
]
